#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5497" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\nII. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\nIII. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" :question/worked-solution "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/id #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" :question/math-academy-id "e-5498" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\nII. $B +4I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/60/6031351ba073c1da6df2d7e96ef1764eff8d6e15d1d1c388e06edf68dc6e9083.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124417 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-8606" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/08/084da4d2f1ef5b0a2359a9a6b7582daf57f619842453fd911233eb004862b941.svg)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dd0d4fb-280a-550e-a592-ead821179211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487590} {:answer/id #uuid "1e87bc2e-1fa6-5dfe-a12d-f3281ca37a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} {:answer/id #uuid "79c00a2a-a33d-50c2-9a06-fe7d080d7095" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487806} {:answer/id #uuid "89763caf-f10d-5fe3-91a2-c8336bf4bcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487830} {:answer/id #uuid "ff51444c-16eb-5add-9d3a-db4575d4b382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e87bc2e-1fa6-5dfe-a12d-f3281ca37a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487628} :answer-field/id #uuid "93a75aae-536a-560a-b748-38762d5ef8b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835" :question/math-academy-id "q-140839" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e0031aa-3dbc-5f68-93e0-bb2ed010e74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123027} {:answer/id #uuid "d0573792-ad45-544b-94fd-b9d9148f8136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123028} {:answer/id #uuid "e56fa8f8-674f-55cb-bba7-ff19538337c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123029} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d1c10-aa15-5508-bc23-2f0ebdf5ce51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123030} {:answer/id #uuid "54341bfe-9ab7-5883-9acb-5a53daf0e5db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123031}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e79e35d-b84d-52e4-966a-93f9f1835803" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123026}] :question/id #uuid "e5a631d9-8e9c-5aa8-b5b9-8e752a0ab9bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37423" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3 \\cdot 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A$?\n\n1. $λ(λ + 2)(λ - 1) = 0$\n2. $λ^{3} + 3λ^{2} - 2λ + 4 = 0$\n3. $λ^{3} - 5λ^{2} - 10λ = 0$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47e1bc98-e7e4-52f8-984d-07090fcce509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448608} {:answer/id #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448609} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cef80fb-6ab7-552e-906b-93d05bf07abc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449344} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab6b7cce-8578-54ae-a81c-3138c19f494d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448610} {:answer/id #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0a10d530d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0a10d530d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c3d2be-b0df-5bbf-8142-98edc40dc42f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449345} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0254d36b-a197-5a46-943b-99f03cefa2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbfda1-4699-5f8e-b698-221bd7d85dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0254d36b-a197-5a46-943b-99f03cefa2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448612} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5503ea3-b219-5b46-b79b-0e271f4f1b90" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-37523" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1fef96-a4b5-56a3-ae12-1ad088781515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448702} {:answer/id #uuid "c988276e-ae24-58e1-b3c3-71288a68bb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c988276e-ae24-58e1-b3c3-71288a68bb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448703} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cfef4ec-bf41-5b26-a871-8eaa14e4c137" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449391} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fab0312d-2e7a-525e-ba6d-532165dc1bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} {:answer/id #uuid "46330574-7aae-5314-b20d-1471b582f872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab0312d-2e7a-525e-ba6d-532165dc1bc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448704} :answer-field/id #uuid "004390c1-de50-56c3-839a-de91fd3c41a3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449392} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7cdfa98-4044-5fa7-991a-cdd2d17905fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ebdbcd-7ff6-5904-beff-00069d922192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7cdfa98-4044-5fa7-991a-cdd2d17905fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448706} :answer-field/id #uuid "872a7bbb-a3ff-52ad-be3b-b42dbd804cf8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" :question/math-academy-id "q-47568" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc28d24-2966-5404-9657-12db361e583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb9ebe2-2b9f-520b-ae5c-3a3b086533b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448835}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc28d24-2966-5404-9657-12db361e583f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448834} :answer-field/id #uuid "da7a49a0-682e-5485-8704-c21a8df81e3a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449457} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8f791e7-bb86-5a9f-b7a4-d3cc7968a9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448836} {:answer/id #uuid "d1d857bb-3ddc-59a7-a9e1-b62a1bf5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1d857bb-3ddc-59a7-a9e1-b62a1bf5bbc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448837} :answer-field/id #uuid "979415cb-cf88-5d43-b613-b0e7364d110e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449458} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4cb6286-7e1d-5d71-95bf-438c63766041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448838} {:answer/id #uuid "c7255ac2-2e51-5f09-8fcc-64d6f0d0e25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7255ac2-2e51-5f09-8fcc-64d6f0d0e25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448839} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da59e2a-2004-5754-a752-61aa8752b4c9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" :question/math-academy-id "q-97051" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- **II.** $A+2I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer