17592186471794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9fb77f4-4254-5c73-953b-7f787e0f61d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471794} :answer-field/id #uuid "74391771-4e6a-57d2-9134-21c7fb4ea916" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f94c3089-795c-58b5-a020-124bd7b98fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97859" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ non-invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $B?$\n- **I.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-4\\lambda =0$\n- **II.** ${\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda +4=0$\n- **III.** $\\lambda (\\lambda +4)(\\lambda -1)=0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ is non-invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ must be an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $B$ since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $B.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a10545-2e6a-5410-8600-0c239ab36b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477811} {:answer/id #uuid "6a09fece-7821-5290-ba7c-33c8642353c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477951} {:answer/id #uuid "75d3c0ba-ecc5-531e-a32d-4aad91e28a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477982} {:answer/id #uuid "8b187d27-74f7-5cce-9dc4-b36648121255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478018} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb064b3-880e-5a8f-a066-a9c976e3909b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b187d27-74f7-5cce-9dc4-b36648121255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478018} :answer-field/id #uuid "e13459e6-807d-540e-845c-f7ab2efc8cf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc0c0d74-29c3-5a54-b40b-f1888e3dbce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-97861" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $C?$\n\nI. ${\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda =0$\n\nII. $\\lambda (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +3)=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{4}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+4=0$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could  be the characteristic equation of $C.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc8307-77bd-54e5-ae5b-b3521133ccfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a42069-f918-5e71-9dd9-2a211c78e924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452437} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b11ffe1-abc8-57b0-8f5c-136583ab3529" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453066} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd9b746-af0b-579f-9539-7f4c89d3f3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452439} {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8139ae7-a53d-5b72-bf22-02323cba44f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452440} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab27423b-0ac8-5d39-97b6-dba386cf4d90" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} {:answer/id #uuid "e535c4d0-7baa-5a48-acbd-a5afaa7c103d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab3cdd13-53bd-57d7-9494-64f83f7502ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452441} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a2becec-7b1e-57b8-92f8-0a9e0aaf26ff" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed9c37d8-c5de-5898-be3f-6a097e562da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97897" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $Bx=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B-0I=B.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B-0I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $M=B$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203890e4-890c-5526-9730-446dc08c0036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452455} {:answer/id #uuid "e1927c58-05f9-55ce-b818-86f89e6aed6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "203890e4-890c-5526-9730-446dc08c0036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452455} :answer-field/id #uuid "6053ca1b-7b17-52f7-b96e-1178abc2441c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ac890e2-062b-5ce0-94f1-12d35c3cbe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452457} {:answer/id #uuid "2885df9a-f2bd-5273-b75d-30f8d8cff1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ac890e2-062b-5ce0-94f1-12d35c3cbe80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452457} :answer-field/id #uuid "37352a86-32ee-56e1-ad55-a35f40ca3534" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcbf4bbb-220b-5800-806a-bc2f67c879d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452459} {:answer/id #uuid "a278fb21-91c2-538a-a6ed-06ef96a955ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a278fb21-91c2-538a-a6ed-06ef96a955ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452460} :answer-field/id #uuid "248362ca-1efd-54c1-b856-0618881c44aa" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23a6bd8a-b3a8-5b1e-9289-48d8854d334a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97948" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C-5I$ is a non-singular matrix.\n- **II.** $\\text{rank}(C-5I)=3$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(C-5I)\\ne 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-5I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f28abe1-7ce7-5fcd-800c-5355d28b4dd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454274} {:answer/id #uuid "975b6d1f-67e4-5ad0-a60c-f212c150bba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975b6d1f-67e4-5ad0-a60c-f212c150bba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454275} :answer-field/id #uuid "01e4ad4b-797b-5e1c-957c-e3b4e3622e5b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454887} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b268ef17-aa66-5222-b880-47a780072089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454276} {:answer/id #uuid "98e68f38-5fa0-5696-a788-a965baf32116" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b268ef17-aa66-5222-b880-47a780072089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454276} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0d8911-05a3-5867-85d8-d03e8d8a263a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454888} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9e6411f-f645-554b-849c-9e1f5f16f32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454278} {:answer/id #uuid "0df6ff7c-41ab-51b6-a2ff-1f6813596ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0df6ff7c-41ab-51b6-a2ff-1f6813596ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454279} :answer-field/id #uuid "8031e5cb-78c7-579e-9e68-cf112703c2a4" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a33206bb-7969-5524-a745-290fffeff16f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97967" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- **II.** $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3506dab1-62ad-546f-b8d5-2e43cdacdb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477838} {:answer/id #uuid "4b9ffb8b-392e-547a-b8dd-0ab8af442d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477886} {:answer/id #uuid "91eb4fef-125c-5602-af85-46700f3acf65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478023} {:answer/id #uuid "9b33856e-57bb-575b-a78c-287c2948dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} {:answer/id #uuid "c64ada4d-645e-5dde-adf5-9d7a1b8e974e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b33856e-57bb-575b-a78c-287c2948dabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478034} :answer-field/id #uuid "754fcbaf-4a94-546a-a360-fa1f3050ad24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e4997f39-9351-5840-b1b4-1804fe6c9f0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97971" :question/problem "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} {:answer/id #uuid "478fc76b-1ffb-5eba-9444-fb9a5daab721" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2408efc0-effa-5d1a-95bf-4e5888eb4e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452485} :answer-field/id #uuid "298cd9f6-0f4b-5d91-b1e2-380da3c049cf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453090} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} {:answer/id #uuid "194e8931-2c02-5f42-b19b-625e045d7857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890097e8-1b87-5c8b-80f8-73adc10117b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452487} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5a7b14-0732-5180-838e-5db043472ec8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453091} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88db7284-406b-5896-82c0-024eb4192579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452489} {:answer/id #uuid "35417b18-69fa-55aa-bd77-4e154b50f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35417b18-69fa-55aa-bd77-4e154b50f8f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452490} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d73097d-9167-5ae9-ac40-5d49927706d1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "89915cc7-c25f-5711-a62f-10d7e3f1b8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97972" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)={R}^{2}$\n- **II.** $C-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(C-2I) & =2-\\text{nullity}(C-2I) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C-2I$ is singular, then $(C-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0736bf-0992-5540-92e4-4db3dc860d7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4ccb0fb-57b3-54cc-9a0e-7b3b2ec086c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452491} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a2ad23-7a6f-5d92-beff-9522a7a1c6e4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453093} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4c9d99e-8bcf-530d-a35b-845c74343724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452493} {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b94d0759-6f74-5338-954d-fc2d4ed8aa4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452494} :answer-field/id #uuid "eabb1107-ac4d-5f76-9ad8-0708cf4bdbd2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453094} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee5ed75-acac-56ef-98ed-57d5822de8c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452495} {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537989db-6a16-5fcc-aa37-3d4aa93c947c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452496} :answer-field/id #uuid "86f83e84-79e0-5f94-a3f0-7b675d59f092" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "68bc418d-6421-5050-b933-2a80a591867e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97975" :question/problem "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(C+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Null}(C+3I)={0}$\n- **II.** $C+3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(C+3I) & =4-\\text{rank}(C+3I) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(C+3I)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(C+3I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $C-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=C+3I$ is singular, then $(C+3I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $C.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}]}
